## Report
Validated the finding in the Python codebase with targeted source review plus a dynamic PoC and non-interactive pdb trace. Crash validation is not applicable because this is an authorization/visibility information disclosure, not memory corruption; valgrind is also not applicable/unavailable (`valgrind not installed`). Relevant existing tests passed: `pytest -q tests/test_whatsapp_rag_orchestrator.py tests/test_rag_retrieval.py tests/test_knowledge_documents.py` -> `39 passed`. Evidence: the WhatsApp webhook POST path calls `orchestrate_inbound_message` after inserting an inbound message at `app/api/v1/routes.py:3245-3262`, making the new RAG path reachable through inbound WhatsApp messages. The schema defines `visibility` including `agents_only` at `infra/postgres/01-schema.sql:288-299`. The orchestrator knowledge query at `app/services/rag_orchestrator.py:119-130` selects `kd.visibility` but filters only `kc.tenant_id=$1` and `kd.status='active'`; there is no exclusion of `agents_only`. If RAG finds sufficient context, `app/services/rag_orchestrator.py:141-154` sends the bot reply without checking match visibility. `app/services/rag_retrieval.py:158-163` embeds `chunk_excerpt(best.chunk_text)` in the answer, so selected chunk text is disclosed. The PoC fake asyncpg connection returns exactly one active `agents_only` chunk containing `DESCUENTO INTERNO VIP 80% codigo STAFF-ONLY-123`. The run output shows `VISIBILITY_FILTER_PRESENT= False`, `RESULT= {'action': 'bot_replied', ...}`, `SECRET_DISCLOSED= True`, and `TOP_VISIBILITY= agents_only`. The pdb trace stopped at `rag_orchestrator.py:141` before `_send_bot_reply` and printed `decision` containing the secret-bearing answer, `matches[0].visibility == 'agents_only'`, and the secret chunk text. This confirms the externally-triggered orchestrator can construct and queue a WhatsApp bot reply from an agent-only knowledge document.

